---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Marginalia from '../components/Marginalia.astro';

// The full arc, written as a narrative rather than a résumé. Facts come
// from Erin's LinkedIn export. All copy is draft for Erin to rewrite,
// especially the AI section, which BRAND.md asks to be clearly marked.
---

<Base
  title="About | Erin Ankeny"
  description="Twenty years of getting technical teams and everyone else to understand each other, from technical writing to program leadership to founding-team operations."
>
  <div class="content">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <figure class="headshot">
        <span class="placeholder">[PLACEHOLDER: headshot, no photo exists yet]</span>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The short version: I've spent twenty years being the person who gets
        groups with different vocabularies working from the same page. The
        titles have changed. The job hasn't.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Where it started</h2>
      <div class="annotated">
        <Marginalia>I also employed the serial comma.</Marginalia>
        <p>
          I started as a technical writer, first at Raytheon Solipsys and later
          at Oracle, turning what engineers know into something the rest of the
          world can use. Manuals, training, usability testing. It taught me the
          skill everything since has depended on: listening to one group
          carefully enough to explain them to another.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The translation job, scaled up</h2>
      <p>
        At Gloto, a mobile and web studio, I ran client services and then
        operations as EVP, serving as the communications hub between customers,
        engineering, and management for brands like Marvel, the NBA, and NBC
        Universal.
      </p>
      <p>
        At TD Ameritrade I was the Scrum Master and project manager for the
        MarTech Enablement and Data Architecture Lab, coordinating delivery
        across two teams and keeping communication moving across the
        enterprise. I stayed through the Charles Schwab acquisition as a senior
        IT program manager. Later, at Humana, I coached marketing operations
        teams through an agile transformation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The startup years</h2>
      <p>
        In 2021 I joined the founding team of Sentinel Insights, a platform
        that helps teams make sure their marketing technologies are doing what
        they're supposed to. For five years I ran product operations and
        customer support, built the team, and did whatever else the day
        required. In parallel I ran my own consultancy, Project 3 Consulting,
        taking on program management, agile and digital transformation, web
        development, and MarTech work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>
        Today I'm a technical program manager at Red Hat, and I build websites
        and brands for small businesses on the side, with property and home
        services as the corner I know best. I've worked inside those
        businesses, not just built pages for them: I ran the marketing function
        for a family-owned home inspection company for roughly seven years, and
        I've been a licensed realtor since 2016, still handling{' '}
        <a href="https://realtyconnect.net/site/erin-ankeny/">relocation referrals</a>{' '}
        through a nationwide network.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How I work with AI</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="placeholder">[DRAFT: Erin to rewrite this section in her own words]</span>
      </p>
      <div class="annotated">
        <Marginalia>
          This site is the example: planned by a person, drafted with a model,
          and reviewed line by line.
        </Marginalia>
        <p>
          I treat AI the way I've treated every technical team I've worked
          with: as a capable party that doesn't share my vocabulary yet. The
          work is deciding which parts of a project belong to a human and which
          to a model, being precise about what I'm asking for, and checking
          what comes back. This site was built that way. I wrote the brief and
          the design rules, a model wrote the first pass of the code and copy,
          and every line got a human read before it shipped.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-intro {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-48);
  }

  .page-intro p {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
    font-size: var(--size-subhead);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  /* Reserved space for the headshot. Swaps to an img when one exists. */
  .headshot {
    margin-top: var(--space-24);
    width: 200px;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    background: var(--panel);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: var(--space-16);
    text-align: center;
  }

  section {
    margin-top: var(--space-48);
  }

  section p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .page-intro {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 200px;
      gap: var(--space-16) var(--space-48);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .page-intro h1 {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .page-intro p {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .headshot {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / 3;
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .page-intro {
      padding-block-start: var(--space-64);
    }

    section {
      margin-top: var(--space-64);
    }
  }
</style>
